---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Break the API" hint="Something goes wrong for 30 seconds. Choose what, then turn Stripe's protections on and off and watch who gets an error, how hard the workers are pushed and whether customers are charged twice. A simple model with round numbers, not Stripe's code or data.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b>. A runaway script sends <b>5,000</b> requests a second for 30 seconds and nothing protects the API. <b>41.1%</b> of customers' requests fail, the busiest second is <b>5.6</b> times what the workers can finish, and they are over capacity for <b>30</b> seconds. Charge requests fail just as often (<b>41.1%</b>), because nothing tells them apart from the rest.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Request rate limiter</b> to <b>Token bucket per user</b>: failures fall to <b>0.2%</b> and the busiest second to <b>1.1</b> times capacity, with just <b>1</b> second over. That second is the burst the bucket allows before it starts to reject.</li>
    <li>Now set <b>What goes wrong</b> to <b>Workers slow down</b>. They can finish only 400 requests a second for 30 seconds, and no single user is to blame, so the rate limiter cannot help: <b>16.7%</b> of requests fail, charges included, and the busiest second is <b>1.5</b> times capacity.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Clients retry</b> to <b>Retry at once</b>. Every failed request is sent again, up to three tries in all, so the busiest second climbs to <b>3.3</b> times capacity. <b>133</b> charges are made twice, because a charge that finished but lost its answer is sent again and charged a second time.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Idempotency keys</b> to <b>Keys sent with every charge</b>: the doubled charges go from <b>133</b> to <b>0</b>, because a retry of a finished charge is answered from its saved result. The load hardly changes and <b>14.9%</b> of requests still fail, since keys make retries safe but do not make the workers faster.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Load shedder</b> to <b>Reserve capacity for charges</b>: the charge requests that fail drop from <b>14.9%</b> to <b>0.0%</b> and the busiest second falls to <b>0.9</b> times capacity, though <b>17.3%</b> of all requests still fail, because the non-critical ones are given up first.</li>
    <li>Finally set <b>What goes wrong</b> back to <b>A runaway script</b>. With everything switched on, only <b>0.3%</b> of requests fail, no charge fails (<b>0.0%</b>), the workers peak at <b>0.7</b> times capacity and nobody is charged twice.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg stp" data-stp>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>What goes wrong <select class="pg-in" data-incident><option value="runawayScript">A runaway script</option><option value="slowWorkers">Workers slow down</option></select></label>
      <label>Request rate limiter <select class="pg-in" data-limiter><option value="off">Not used</option><option value="on">Token bucket per user</option></select></label>
      <label>Load shedder <select class="pg-in" data-shedder><option value="off">Not used</option><option value="on">Reserve capacity for charges</option></select></label>
      <label>Clients retry <select class="pg-in" data-retry><option value="none">Never</option><option value="immediate">Retry at once</option><option value="backoff">Wait longer each time, with jitter</option></select></label>
      <label>Idempotency keys <select class="pg-in" data-idem><option value="off">Not used</option><option value="on">Keys sent with every charge</option></select></label>
      <button type="button" class="btn" data-reset>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Customers who saw an error</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Charge requests that failed</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Busiest second at the workers</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b4><h3>Charges made twice</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o4></span><span class="pg-note" data-n4></span></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="stp-chart" viewBox="0 0 600 130" role="img" data-chart preserveAspectRatio="none">
      <rect data-incident-band y="0" height="130" fill="var(--bad)" opacity=".08" />
      <line data-capacity-line x1="0" x2="600" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5 4" />
      <polyline data-load fill="none" stroke="var(--role-3)" stroke-width="2.5" />
    </svg>
    <p class="pg-note" data-chart-note>Requests reaching the workers each second, as a multiple of what they can finish (dashed line = 1.0 times). The shaded band is the 30 seconds when something is wrong.</p>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Model, invented for teaching and not Stripe data: a 60 second run in which 600 requests a second come from many customers, 30% of them critical (creating charges) and the rest non-critical (such as listing charges). Workers can finish 1,000 requests a second, or 400 while slowed down. The runaway script is one user sending 5,000 non-critical requests a second. The token bucket refills 100 a second and holds 500. The load shedder lets non-critical requests use half the capacity and keeps the other half for critical ones, and its rejections cost the workers nothing. A request fails when the workers cannot finish it, and a failed request is retried up to twice more, at once (the next second) or spread out over 1 to 2, then 1 to 4 seconds. Of the charges that finish, 2% never get their answer back, so the client treats them as failures and may retry them. Without keys a retried charge is charged again; with keys it is answered from the saved result and costs the workers nothing. Stripe's posts give no production limits, and real systems are more complicated than this.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<style>
  .stp-chart { width: 100%; height: 130px; display: block; background: var(--surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; }
</style>
<script>
  import { query } from '../../playground';
  import { INCIDENT_END, INCIDENT_START, RUN_SECONDS, simulate, type Incident, type RetryPolicy } from '../../models/stripe-api.ts';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-stp]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const CHART_WIDTH = 600, CHART_HEIGHT = 130, CHART_PAD = 8;
    const MIN_CHART_MAX = 2; // the chart always reaches at least twice capacity
    const PERCENT = 100;
    const BAD_ERROR_PERCENT = 5; // an error rate above this turns the box red
    const BAD_LOAD = 1; // more than capacity turns the box red
    const DEFAULTS: Record<string, string> = { incident: 'runawayScript', limiter: 'off', shedder: 'off', retry: 'none', idem: 'off' };
    const select = (key: string) => q<HTMLSelectElement>(`[data-${key}]`);
    const seconds = (n: number) => (n * CHART_WIDTH) / RUN_SECONDS;

    function draw() {
      const result = simulate({
        incident: select('incident').value as Incident, limiter: select('limiter').value === 'on', shedder: select('shedder').value === 'on',
        retry: select('retry').value as RetryPolicy, idempotency: select('idem').value === 'on',
      });
      const failed = PERCENT * result.failedShare; const criticalFailed = PERCENT * result.criticalFailedShare;
      const duplicates = Math.round(result.duplicateCharges);
      q('[data-o1]').textContent = `${failed.toFixed(1)}%`;
      q('[data-n1]').textContent = failed > 0 ? 'of all requests ended in an error after the retries' : 'every request worked';
      q('[data-o2]').textContent = `${criticalFailed.toFixed(1)}%`;
      q('[data-n2]').textContent = criticalFailed > 0 ? 'customers could not create charges' : 'charges kept working';
      q('[data-o3]').textContent = `${result.peakLoad.toFixed(1)} times capacity`;
      q('[data-n3]').textContent = `${result.overloadedSeconds} second${result.overloadedSeconds === 1 ? '' : 's'} over capacity`;
      q('[data-o4]').textContent = String(duplicates);
      q('[data-n4]').textContent = duplicates > 0 ? 'a finished charge whose answer was lost was charged again on retry' : 'no customer was charged twice';
      const bad = [failed > BAD_ERROR_PERCENT, criticalFailed > 0, result.peakLoad > BAD_LOAD, duplicates > 0];
      ['b1', 'b2', 'b3', 'b4'].forEach((b, i) => q<HTMLElement>(`[data-${b}]`).style.setProperty('--c', bad[i] ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--good)'));

      const top = Math.max(MIN_CHART_MAX, result.peakLoad * 1.05);
      const y = (v: number) => CHART_HEIGHT - CHART_PAD - (v / top) * (CHART_HEIGHT - 2 * CHART_PAD);
      q('[data-load]').setAttribute('points', result.loadBySecond.map((v, i) => `${seconds(i + 0.5).toFixed(1)},${y(v).toFixed(1)}`).join(' '));
      const line = q('[data-capacity-line]'); line.setAttribute('y1', y(1).toFixed(1)); line.setAttribute('y2', y(1).toFixed(1));
      const band = q('[data-incident-band]'); band.setAttribute('x', String(seconds(INCIDENT_START))); band.setAttribute('width', String(seconds(INCIDENT_END - INCIDENT_START)));
      q('[data-chart]').setAttribute('aria-label', `Load on the workers over ${RUN_SECONDS} seconds, peaking at ${result.peakLoad.toFixed(1)} times what they can finish`);

      const out = q('[data-verdict]');
      const broken = failed > BAD_ERROR_PERCENT || criticalFailed > 0 || result.peakLoad > BAD_LOAD || duplicates > 0;
      out.classList.toggle('bad', broken);
      if (!broken) out.textContent = `The API held: ${failed.toFixed(1)}% of requests failed, charges kept working, the workers never went over capacity and no customer was charged twice.`;
      else if (duplicates > 0) out.textContent = `${duplicates} customers were charged twice, because a retry repeated a charge that had already worked. Turn idempotency keys on.`;
      else if (criticalFailed > 0 && result.peakLoad > BAD_LOAD) out.textContent = `Charges failed for ${criticalFailed.toFixed(1)}% of requests and the workers were pushed to ${result.peakLoad.toFixed(1)} times capacity. Try the protection that fits what went wrong: the rate limiter for a runaway script, the load shedder when capacity is short.`;
      else out.textContent = `${failed.toFixed(1)}% of requests failed, though charges kept working, and the workers peaked at ${result.peakLoad.toFixed(1)} times capacity.`;
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    q('[data-reset]').addEventListener('click', () => {
      Object.keys(DEFAULTS).forEach((k) => (select(k).value = DEFAULTS[k]));
      draw();
    });
    draw();
  });
</script>
